          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 50-year-old man develops sudden, violent flinging of his left arm and leg. A s</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 50-year-old man develops sudden, violent flinging of his left arm and leg. A small infarct is seen on imaging. Which structure is most likely damaged?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Left subthalamic nucleus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hemiballismus is contralateral to the lesion, so flinging of the left limb points to a right-sided lesion. A left subthalamic nucleus lesion would cause flinging of the right limb.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Right subthalamic nucleus</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The subthalamic nucleus normally excites the internal globus pallidus to restrain unwanted movement. Its destruction releases uncontrolled movement on the opposite side of the body, which is hemiballismus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Right caudate nucleus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The caudate is part of the striatum. Striatal lesions more typically produce chorea, a flowing rather than violent flinging movement, and this case points to the subthalamic nucleus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Left substantia nigra</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The substantia nigra supplies dopamine to the striatum. Its loss causes Parkinsonism (bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor), not violent flinging of a limb.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Revisit the case. The flinging limb is on the left, so the lesion must lie on the opposite side of the brain, in the subthalamic nucleus."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Right. Each deficit maps to a specific region, and the flinging limb tells you the side of the lesion."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
