          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient follows commands accurately but speaks in slow, effortful, non-fluent </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient follows commands accurately but speaks in slow, effortful, non-fluent sentences after an infarct of the left inferior frontal gyrus. Which area is most likely damaged?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Wernicke's area (area 22) in the superior temporal gyrus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Wernicke's area governs speech comprehension. Its lesion produces fluent but meaningless speech with poor understanding, the opposite of this patient's picture.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Broca's area (areas 44 and 45) in the inferior frontal gyrus</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Broca's area organises speech production. Damage causes non-fluent speech with preserved comprehension, which is Broca's aphasia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Primary motor cortex (area 4) in the precentral gyrus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Area 4 controls voluntary limb movement. Damage causes contralateral weakness, but it does not specifically impair the formation of speech.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Primary visual cortex (area 17) on the calcarine sulcus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Area 17 lies in the occipital lobe. Damage causes contralateral visual field loss and has no direct effect on speech production.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Think about where the speech production area sits. It is in the left inferior frontal gyrus, in front of the motor strip."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Right. The left inferior frontal gyrus holds Broca's area, which produces fluent speech. Comprehension is preserved because Wernicke's area is intact."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
