          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In Parkinson's disease, dopamine is progressively lost from the substantia nigra</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In Parkinson's disease, dopamine is progressively lost from the substantia nigra. What is the consequence for the basal ganglia pathways?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The direct pathway dominates, releasing the thalamus and producing chorea</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is the pattern of Huntington's disease, where the indirect pathway fails first. Chorea is not the characteristic Parkinsonian pattern.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The indirect pathway dominates, suppressing the thalamus and blocking movement</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Dopamine normally facilitates the direct pathway and restrains the indirect one. Without it, the indirect pathway prevails, and the thalamic suppression produces bradykinesia, rigidity and resting tremor.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hippocampal output through the fornix is blocked</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The fornix and Papez circuit belong to the limbic system, which governs memory and emotion. They are not the basal ganglia pathway that fails in Parkinson's disease.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Relay from the ventrolateral thalamus to the cerebellum fails</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The ventrolateral thalamus coordinates movement, but its failure is not the primary mechanism of Parkinson's disease. The problem lies in the striatal pathway balance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Recall the two pathways. Dopamine facilitates the direct pathway, so losing it lets the indirect pathway dominate."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Right. The loss of dopamine tips the balance of the direct and indirect pathways, and that imbalance causes the Parkinsonian features."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
