          "label": "<p>A berry aneurysm at which vessel of the circle of Willis is most likely to compr</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A berry aneurysm at which vessel of the circle of Willis is most likely to compress the oculomotor nerve, producing a fixed dilated pupil and a drooping eyelid?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anterior communicating artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is the commonest site for a berry aneurysm, but it bridges the two anterior cerebral arteries across the midline. It does not run beside the oculomotor nerve.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Posterior communicating artery</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The posterior communicating artery links the internal carotid to the posterior cerebral artery and lies beside CN III. An aneurysm here compresses the nerve, giving ptosis and a fixed dilated pupil.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Middle cerebral artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The middle cerebral artery is the largest branch of the internal carotid and supplies the lateral hemisphere. It is the commonest stroke territory, but it does not compress CN III.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Posterior cerebral artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The posterior cerebral artery arises from the basilar artery and supplies the occipital lobe, thalamus and medial temporal lobe. The narration places the CN III compression with the posterior communicating artery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Think about the vessel that connects the internal carotid to the posterior circulation. It runs close to the oculomotor nerve."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Right. Knowing which vessel lies beside which cranial nerve lets you predict the clinical signs of an aneurysm."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
